# Elasticsearch / OpenSearch investigation guidance

This toolset queries an Elasticsearch or OpenSearch cluster that stores logs, traces,
or other observability data. Treat those indices as a primary source of evidence, not
a last resort.

## When a Kubernetes object is missing, still query the indices

A user may ask about a workload by its Kubernetes name (a deployment, pod, service,
statefulset, etc.) whose logs or traces are shipped to this cluster. The Kubernetes
object may no longer exist: the pod was deleted or replaced, the deployment was torn
down, or the workload only ever ran elsewhere. A `kubectl get`/`describe` returning
nothing does NOT mean there is no evidence.

If a Kubernetes lookup for the named workload comes back empty, do NOT conclude "not
found" and stop. Query these indices for the same name before answering. The indexed
logs and traces frequently outlive the Kubernetes object and are often the only
surviving record of what happened. Search by the workload name across the relevant
fields (for example `service`, `serviceName`, `kubernetes.pod_name`, `pod`, or a
`message` substring), widening the field set if the first attempt returns nothing.

## Discover before assuming

Index names and field names vary between clusters. When you don't already know where a
workload's data lives, list indices and inspect mappings first, then query. Don't guess
a single index name, get zero hits, and give up. Confirm the data isn't there by
checking the indices that plausibly hold it.
